 B) When you must consider whether the law or safety requires you to act:
 Some roles require specific reports, and some situations may require a supervisor to assess safety. Two common New York examples:
 
-Child abuse or maltreatment: Certain professionals are mandated reporters under Social Services Law §413 and must make a report to the Statewide Central Register when the law's threshold is met — for example, when you have reasonable cause to suspect child abuse or maltreatment while acting in your professional capacity [M02-5]. Many agencies make all staff mandated reporters by policy even if the law does not. You do not need to prove abuse, and you do not investigate on your own — you follow your agency's reporting steps and call the register [M02-5].
-
-Harm to self or others: Agencies have specific safety protocols when someone may be at imminent risk of harm. Frontline staff generally do not decide on their own to break confidentiality to warn an identifiable third party — they immediately involve a supervisor, clinical lead, or safety team who can assess imminence, check relevant state law and case law, and decide what, if anything, must be shared to protect safety, using the least amount of private information necessary [M02-4][M02-8]. In New York, duties related to threats and protective actions are role-specific and fact-specific — follow your supervisor and your agency's safety plan [M02-4][M02-8].
+Child abuse or maltreatment — two different bases, different steps:
+
+Statutory mandated reporters: People listed in Social Services Law §413(1)(a) — such as physicians, nurses, social workers, teachers, police, child care providers, and others in that law's list — are required by law while acting in their professional capacity to immediately make a report or cause a report to be made to the Statewide Central Register when they have reasonable cause to suspect child abuse or maltreatment [M02-5]. The law says: No school, school official, child care provider, foster care provider, residential care facility provider, hospital, medical institution provider or mental health facility provider shall impose any conditions, including prior approval or prior notification, upon a member of their staff specifically required to report under this title [M02-5]. In everyday language: your employer cannot require you to get permission or to tell a supervisor before you report — do not wait for approval. After you report or cause a report, you must immediately notify the person in charge of your institution after the report was made (and if you are the person in charge, you must ensure the report was made) [M02-5]. If you ask a supervisor to make the report for you — that is, you “cause a report to be made (for example: ensuring that a supervisor makes the report)” as described at NYC Administration for Children’s Services [M02-5] — you must still ensure the report was actually made, get the Call I.D., and ensure you are named in the report if more than one staff had reasonable cause — simply telling a supervisor is not equivalent to ensuring a report occurred [M02-5]. You do not need to prove abuse, and you do not investigate on your own [M02-5].
+
+Policy-based reporting responsibility: Many agencies require all staff to report by agency policy even when the law does not list them as statutory reporters [M02-5]. That creates a workplace reporting duty, not a law-based mandated-reporter status — it does not change the law for statutory reporters and does not itself create statutory immunity. If you are reporting only because agency policy says “all staff report,” follow your agency handbook steps — which may include telling a supervisor first — but that policy cannot be applied to delay a statutory reporter’s immediate legal duty above.
+
+Harm to self or others — a different rule with a different legal basis: When someone may be at imminent risk of serious harm to self or others (for example, a detailed plan to hurt an identifiable person tonight), agencies have safety protocols that are separate from child-abuse reporting. In this imminent-risk domain, frontline staff generally do not make solo decisions to break confidentiality to warn an identifiable third party — they immediately involve a supervisor, clinical lead, or safety team who assesses imminence, checks relevant state law and case law, and decides what, if anything, must be shared to protect safety, using the least amount of private information necessary [M02-4][M02-8]. In New York, duties related to threats and protective actions are role-specific and fact-specific — follow your supervisor and your agency's safety plan [M02-4][M02-8]. This supervisor-involvement rule for imminent-risk safety is not the same as the “report immediately without prior approval” rule for child abuse — keep the two domains separate.
 
 C) What "share only what is needed" means:
 Even when sharing is allowed or required, share the minimum necessary to do your job. A new coworker asking for Elena's full intake form "to see her address" does not need her whole history to drop off flyers — they need a flyer list without private health details, or a supervisor-approved outreach plan [M02-1][M02-3].
