+Example 5 — Possible mandated report — statutory vs policy (same situation, two duties). While working with a 10-year-old's family, a parent says, "Sometimes I leave her home alone overnight." If Sam is a statutory mandated reporter listed in Social Services Law §413(1)(a) and has reasonable cause to suspect abuse or maltreatment while acting in professional capacity, Sam has a personal legal duty: immediately make a report or cause a report to be made to the Statewide Central Register when the threshold is met — do not wait for prior approval or prior notification from a supervisor, which the law prohibits as a condition [M02-5]; then immediately notify the person in charge after the report was made. If Sam asks a supervisor to call for Sam (cause a report — “for example: ensuring that a supervisor makes the report” per NYC ACS [M02-5]), Sam must ensure the report was actually made, obtain the Call I.D., and ensure Sam is named if more than one staff had reasonable cause — simply telling a supervisor without ensuring the report occurred does not satisfy the duty [M02-5]. If Sam is reporting only because agency policy says “all staff report” and the law does not list Sam, Sam follows agency handbook steps — which may include telling a supervisor first — but that policy does not change the statutory rule above. In either case: ensure immediate safety, do not investigate alone or promise “I won’t report,” and afterward document facts (date, time, what was said, who was contacted, Call I.D. if obtained) without opinions [M02-5].
 
